#
# SettingsHelper (/usr/libexec/SettingsHelper, nextbsd/nextbsd#628) checks
# the name and password the settings apps' lock asks for with this
# service, as Darwin's authd does with its own pam.d/authorization. It runs
# as root, so pam_self (which /etc/pam.d/login lists) would let anyone in
# and is left out. There is no session: nobody logs in.
#
# nullok, as in /etc/pam.d/system: an administrator whose account has no
# password (the default "admin") authenticates with an empty one. The
# settings apps show such an admin "Allow changes?" with no password field.
# Remove nullok here to make every settings change need a real password.

# auth
auth		required	pam_unix.so		no_warn nullok

# account
account		required	pam_unix.so
